\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{A five-part compatibility datum}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{A \newvocab{compatibility datum} is a tuple
##{(G,\mathbb K,\rho,\alpha,\sigma,C).}
Here #{G} is a group, #{\mathbb K\in\{\mathbb R,\mathbb C\}} is
its coefficient field, and #{\rho:G\to GL_{\mathbb K}(F)} is a
finite-dimensional representation on a fiber #{F}. The map
#{\alpha} is an action of #{G} on a finite graph substrate, in
the sense of a \vocabk{representation datum}{fdip-007H}. The selector
#{\sigma} is an idempotent in a declared algebra of operators on
#{F}, or on a finite direct sum of such fibers. The reference
comparison #{C} is a stated homomorphism, quotient, or numerical
identification against one named object. The coefficient field
is scalar data for #{F}. It is not a residual gauge group.
When #{\mathbb K=\mathbb R}, a complex representation enters
only after realification, as a real representation on the
underlying real vector space. A direct sum of that real fiber
with another real fiber is then one real representation.}

\p{The datum is tested against 5 criteria.}
\ul{
  \li{Exactness. The group, the field, the representation and the
  substrate action are all specified. An inclusion into a matrix
  group is not a substitute for the missing slot.}
  \li{Declared maps. Each comparison with a Dirac, Lorentz, gauge
  or gravitational structure names the homomorphism, kernel, cover
  or branching rule that is actually used.}
  \li{Separation. A spinor or Dirac action is identified with a
  gravitational action only when a separate compatibility law is
  proved. Sharing a name, or an inclusion into #{SU(2)}, is not
  that law.}
  \li{Selector effect. The selector is given, and there is a proof
  of the change it makes to the operator or parameter named in the
  datum.}
  \li{Reference recovery. The comparison names the ordinary object
  recovered in the chosen reference sector, or it records that the
  datum claims no such recovery.}
}

\p{For a fixed datum, each criterion is marked proved, conditional
or open. Proved means the finite obligation stated in that criterion
is established. Conditional means a stated part is established and
the complementary part is unresolved. Open means the required map
or recovery is not constructed. An open mark records that absence.
It is not a theorem that no later construction exists.}
